From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Paslawski <chris@paslawski.me>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:49:35 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] android: Declare and write full range YUV for camera streams

The YUV post-processor converted the software ISP output with
libyuv::ARGBToNV12, limited range BT.601, while the HAL declared JFIF
(full range BT.601) for YCbCr_420_888 streams and left the dataspace
the client asked for on IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED ones, which the display
took as full range BT.709 for the preview. Consumers decoded the data
with the wrong range and matrix, and the preview had a green cast that
the JPEG, encoded straight from RGB, did not have.

Declare JFIF for IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED streams, and limited range
BT.601 for video encoding, which keeps the conversion it had. Convert
RGB to NV12 in the range the stream declares: full range through
libyuv::ARGBToJ420, as libyuv has no full range NV12 conversion. Encode
the JPEG thumbnail full range too, as libjpeg takes it as JFIF.

The AIDL session reports the dataspace the HAL chose and keeps the
requested one, as the next configuration repeats it for the streams it
keeps.

---
Base-commit: 3becb16c1fa22d79a4d7eeb75a36fc895b4b7abf
Source: https://github.com/alessandro-fontana/android_external_libcamera/commit/f3e07c1e1c84d70766343566aba7d72003f88974

